 ## Overview
-The Gradient Descent Solver is a matrix-free optimization method used to update voxel grid parameters in differentiable volume rendering, supporting both constant update rates and the Adam optimizer [S1, S2, unofficial]. It addresses gradient noise and anisotropy by filtering gradients temporally using exponential moving averages, providing stable behavior for ill-conditioned systems [S1, unofficial][S3]. Unlike direct solvers that form normal equations, it accumulates gradients via atomic additions, making it suitable for large-scale or rank-deficient problems where memory efficiency is critical [S1, unofficial].
+The Gradient Descent Solver is a matrix-free optimization method used to update voxel grid parameters in differentiable volume rendering, supporting both constant update rates and the Adam optimizer [S1, S2, unofficial]. It addresses gradient noise and anisotropy by filtering gradients temporally using exponential moving averages, providing stable behavior for ill-conditioned systems [S1, unofficial][S3]. Unlike direct solvers that form normal equations, it accumulates gradients via atomic additions, supporting both $l_2$-norm (least squares) and $l_1$-norm (least absolute deviations) loss functions [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:**
-- Parameter vector $\theta \in \mathbb{R}^N$ (e.g., voxel densities or transfer function coefficients).
-- Loss function $L(\theta)$, supporting $L_2$-norm (least squares) or $L_1$-norm (least absolute deviations) [S1, unofficial].
-- Learning rate $\alpha$.
-- Adam hyperparameters: $\beta_1$ (gradient decay), $\beta_2$ (squared gradient decay) [S3].
-
-**Outputs:**
-- Updated parameter vector $\theta_{t+1}$.
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
